- Part B- Luhn checksum validation (Lanz will mostly handle this part, would also still recommend looking at the documents there's more specific details you need to do to check it)
    1. Starting at the rightmost digit, leave that digit unchanged and double every second digit as you move left.
    2. If a doubled value has two digits, add those digits together. Subtracting 9 from a doubled value greater than 9 is equivalent.
    3. Sum all the resulting values, including unchanged digits.
    4. The number passes the Luhn checksum exactly when the sum is divisible by 10.

    - Separate input-format validation from the checksum calculation. Include a clearly named checkLuhn function and any helpers needed. 

    - Implement the calculation yourself rather than calling a checksum library or external service. 

    - Allow the user to check more than one number without restarting the program. Offer a clear exit choice. Reject malformed inputs and allow another attempt; handle end of input cleanly. 
    
    - Do not modify the original input while preparing the checksum calculation
    